      {/* ── SECTION 05: The outcome ───────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <StorySection
        eyebrow="The outcome"
        headline="30+ hours saved for users. 43% conversion for business"
      >
        <div style={{ display: "flex", flexDirection: "column", gap: 16 }}>
          <P>
            After launch, Innspire tracked usage across an initial cohort of
            hotel customers. The results confirmed what the research had
            suggested: the biggest gain was time. Managers who previously
            spent four to six hours a week managing reviews across platforms
            reported getting that same work done in under an hour using
            ReviewMagic. The 30+ hours figure came from a property managing
            multiple hotel brands, where the consolidation value was highest.
          </P>
          <P>
            The business also gained traction within the first few months of launch, leading to over 1746 customer sign ups and a 43% conversion rate within the first 3 months, leading to ReviewMagic being awarded the  "Most Innovative Hospitality Technology Award" by HTNG’s prestigious TechOvation awards 2020.
          </P>
        </div>

        

      </StorySection>


      {/* ── SECTION 07: Conclusion ────────────────────────────────────────── */}
      <StorySection
        eyebrow="Reflection"
        headline="What designing for hospitality operations taught me about simplicity"
      >
        <div style={{ display: "flex", flexDirection: "column", gap: 16 }}>
          <P>
            ReviewMagic was one of the projects where I truly felt the
            weight of a design decision on someone's daily work. Hotel managers
            didn't have time for a learning curve. They needed something that
            worked the first time they touched it and got better the more they
            used it. That constraint made me a more precise designer — it forced
            me to prioritize what appeared on screen and what stayed
            hidden until needed.
          </P>
          <P>
            It also shaped how I think about efficiency as a design goal. Not
            just speed, but the kind of quiet reliability that makes a tool feel
            like an extension of how you already think. When a manager told me
            they hadn't opened TripAdvisor's own dashboard in three weeks, I knew
            we'd built something genuinely useful.
          </P>
        </div>
      </StorySection>
